A||N||10||chris6878||Starter problems... please help...  :(||||5||
Z||000000||chris6878||12-18-1999||12:21 AM||||Hey everyone...<P>I have my newly rebuild engine (v6) running and everything in my 85 SE.  When i turn the ingnition, it wont turn the starter. I have to get under the car and manualy cross the post over on the starter to start my car. I thought it may be the selinoid, change it, and same results..  any suggestions?<P>Also..   my car is idling close to 3,000 rpm at startup...   what would cause this?<P>thanks..<BR>chris||||reg||5
Z||000001||AL||12-18-1999||12:44 AM||||first check your starter, inside where the starter and the flywheel make contact, check to see if that isn't loose.you can shake the starter and if it rattles it's time for a new starter.your idle can be vacuam or aic valve or timein. good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JetroGT||12-18-1999||03:43 AM||||Did they put the shim back in?<BR>Or it could be a dead spot. Try hitting it with a hammer. Don't laugh, this works. It will cause the armature to move a little so it will get some current.||||reg||3
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||12-18-1999||04:46 AM||||The 3k rpm is a vacuum leak, have fun. Could be a simple vacuum hose, (check under the plenum, there is a hose going to the throttle body that sometimes get worked loose during installation. Thats where mine was.) Or an intake gasket slipped when the manifold was put down. Good luck.||||reg||9
Z||000004||fiero56||12-18-1999||09:04 AM||||Ed parks told me there is really only one vaccume line that can cause that high an idle. <BR>Here is what he told me:"virtually the only thing that can cause that high an idle is to not have<BR>the 1/2" line installed at the back of the throttle valve body.<BR>It is at the bottom of the valve body, and will probably require its<BR>removal. It comes off the intake manifold with a rubber elbow, then goes<BR>into the back of the valve body with a steel tube."||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-18-1999||01:23 PM||||better yet..... only 2 things in a car would have a 1/2 inch diam vacuum line. the brake booster line and the PCV valve line.<P>I'm not sure exactly which is being described above, but if it's the brake line, disconnect and cap it. This will eliminate the entire line and booster from being posible leakes. Same for the PCV really. You can run the motor a bit w/o the PCV and not hurt anything. If plugin that large line solve the problem you can trace it down pretty quick.||||reg||1
Z||000006||dogtired||12-18-1999||05:17 PM||||OK from experience I can tell you that a cracked EGR tube will cause high Idle. I could see the crack and when I heled a rag over the crack the idle would be good?<BR>Check it out, just a thought    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I think your starter problem could be in the switch. If it were the starter or the shim, it wouldn't work when you shorted the wires?<BR>Just my thoughts.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> GOOD LUCK||||reg||1
Z||000007||chris6878||12-18-1999||06:53 PM||||Fiero56..  u right..    i had to remove the distributor today to put the gasket under it (it was leaking a little oil)     and there i seen my problem with the high idle..     i didnt have a chance to fix it this evening.. i did partially block it off...  now it idles 1000 rpms... just right..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   but i still need to hook it up where ever it goes..   its located directly behind the distributor.. coming out of the intake... close to the back valve cover...    i have to elbow that goes to it.. but where does it hook up?<P>and the starter...   well..  another story..  i was having to get under the car and cross the post on the starter to start the engine..   but i had an idea.  i put a wire on the same post with the key wire... now all i have to do is touch it to the hot post of the batter and presto... it starts...      so .. all i can figure  is  : the keyswitch itsn't sending power to the starter... and ideas?<P>thanks for all the replies...<BR>chris||||reg||5
Z||000008||Raydar||12-19-1999||12:15 AM||||Chris... You didn't say if you had an automatic or a stick. Are you sure the park/neutral switch (or the clutch switch) is working correctly? If not, you won't get any juice to the starter when you turn the key.||||reg||1
Z||000009||chris6878||12-19-1999||12:31 AM||||It is an auto tranny...     and how would u check the park/neutral switch u are talking of...?   i am guessing this is the switch on the tranny close to the trunk compartment.. with wide plugin..?     Thanks<P><BR>chris||||reg||3
Z||000010||DJRice||12-20-1999||11:52 AM||||I had the lock cylinder in my column go south and the car would light up but not start.  That was a pain in the neck prob for sure...||||reg||1
